dma-resv: bound the unlocked iterator restart loop (fixes #450)

The Pi 500+ desktop wedge that survived every scheduler fix: Xorg spinning
at 100% system time, in the kernel, never sleeping and never returning, so
the X server serviced no client at all -- no clicks, no keys -- while the
screen kept updating from already-queued flips. A forced crash dump caught
the thread exactly:

	#4  dma_fence_get_rcu ()
	#5  dma_resv_wait_timeout ()
	#6  drm_gem_dma_resv_wait ()
	#7  v3d_wait_bo_ioctl ()
	#8  drm_ioctl_kernel ()
	#14 sys_ioctl ()

Not a fence wait -- the GPU was idle with every queue at emit == done. It
is the unlocked fence-list walk itself:

	rcu_read_lock();
	do {
		dma_resv_iter_restart_unlocked(cursor);
		dma_resv_iter_walk_unlocked(cursor);
	} while (dma_resv_fences_list(cursor->obj) != cursor->fences);
	rcu_read_unlock();

The loop restarts whenever the list pointer moved, and it does so inside
the RCU read section. On Linux that converges: a writer publishes the new
list and the old one is freed asynchronously. On FreeBSD rcu_read_lock()
is an epoch section and freeing the old list must wait for the epoch to
drain -- so a reader that never leaves the section blocks the very writer
whose completion would let it converge, while that writer has already
published the new pointer. Reader and writer starve each other and the
loop spins forever.

The writer here is the hardware cursor. Every pointer motion is a
cursor-plane atomic commit adding a fence to the scanout BO's reservation
-- the same reservation v3d_wait_bo_ioctl() is walking. That is why this
only appeared once vc4 started exposing cursor planes, and why forcing
Option "SWcursor" (which removes the writer entirely) made it vanish. A
software cursor is not an acceptable fix, only the experiment that
identified the writer.

Fix: leave the epoch every few restarts and yield, so the writer can drain
and free and the list can settle. Safe at this point and only this point --
cursor->fence is held by reference from dma_fence_get_rcu(), while
cursor->fences is merely RCU-protected and the next statement re-reads it
in dma_resv_iter_restart_unlocked().

No #ifdef __FreeBSD__: drm-kmod builds nowhere else, and the guard would
only leave an unused variable behind.

--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-resv.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-resv.c
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-fence-array.h>
 #include <linux/export.h>
 #include <linux/mm.h>
+#include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/sched/mm.h>
 #include <linux/mmu_notifier.h>
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
@@ -405,6 +406,44 @@
 	} while (true);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Bound the unlocked iterators' restart loop.
+ *
+ * Both iterators re-walk the fence list from the start whenever the list
+ * pointer changed underneath them, and they do it inside rcu_read_lock().
+ * On Linux that terminates: a writer publishes the new list and frees the
+ * old one asynchronously, so the comparison converges.
+ *
+ * On FreeBSD rcu_read_lock() is an epoch section, and freeing the old list
+ * has to wait for that epoch to drain. A reader that never leaves the
+ * section therefore blocks the writer that would let it converge, while the
+ * writer has already published the new pointer -- so the loop spins at 100%
+ * CPU, in the kernel, forever. Measured on a Pi 500+ with a hardware cursor:
+ * every pointer motion is a cursor-plane atomic commit adding a fence to the
+ * scanout BO's reservation, which is the very list v3d_wait_bo_ioctl() is
+ * walking. Xorg wedged in exactly this loop, and a crash dump caught it:
+ *
+ *	dma_fence_get_rcu / dma_resv_wait_timeout / drm_gem_dma_resv_wait
+ *	/ v3d_wait_bo_ioctl / drm_ioctl / sys_ioctl
+ *
+ * The GPU was idle and every job complete; the walker simply could not
+ * finish. So leave the epoch periodically and yield. That lets the writer
+ * drain and free, after which the list settles and the walk converges. It is
+ * safe here and only here: cursor->fence is held by reference via
+ * dma_fence_get_rcu(), while cursor->fences is only RCU-protected -- and the
+ * very next statement re-reads it in dma_resv_iter_restart_unlocked().
+ */
+#define	DMA_RESV_ITER_MAX_SPINS	8
+
+#define	DMA_RESV_ITER_YIELD(spins) do {					\
+	if (++(spins) > DMA_RESV_ITER_MAX_SPINS) {			\
+		rcu_read_unlock();					\
+		cond_resched();						\
+		rcu_read_lock();					\
+		(spins) = 0;						\
+	}								\
+} while (0)
+
 /**
  * dma_resv_iter_first_unlocked - first fence in an unlocked dma_resv obj.
  * @cursor: the cursor with the current position
@@ -419,8 +458,11 @@
  */
 struct dma_fence *dma_resv_iter_first_unlocked(struct dma_resv_iter *cursor)
 {
+	unsigned int spins = 0;
+
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	do {
+		DMA_RESV_ITER_YIELD(spins);
 		dma_resv_iter_restart_unlocked(cursor);
 		dma_resv_iter_walk_unlocked(cursor);
 	} while (dma_resv_fences_list(cursor->obj) != cursor->fences);
@@ -442,14 +484,17 @@
  */
 struct dma_fence *dma_resv_iter_next_unlocked(struct dma_resv_iter *cursor)
 {
+	unsigned int spins = 0;
 	bool restart;
 
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	cursor->is_restarted = false;
 	restart = dma_resv_fences_list(cursor->obj) != cursor->fences;
 	do {
-		if (restart)
+		if (restart) {
+			DMA_RESV_ITER_YIELD(spins);
 			dma_resv_iter_restart_unlocked(cursor);
+		}
 		dma_resv_iter_walk_unlocked(cursor);
 		restart = true;
 	} while (dma_resv_fences_list(cursor->obj) != cursor->fences);
